\begin{abstract}
We predict, at 05:00 on the day of a flight, whether a New York departure will arrive at least 15 minutes late, using only the published schedule, static aircraft data and the delays of earlier days. On <<n(d1['summary']['flights'])>> flights from 2013 we follow the full machine-learning life cycle: problem definition, data collection, EDA, preprocessing, a chronological split, leakage-safe feature engineering and selection, baselines, model selection, honest tuning of gradient boosting with rolling months, and a single evaluation on November--December (base rate <<f3(d5['test_base_rate'])>>). Tuned XGBoost (test PR AUC <<f3(GB['pr_auc'])>>), logistic regression (<<f3(LR['pr_auc'])>>) and a hybrid that boosts from the logistic-regression prediction (<<f3(HY['pr_auc'])>>) all beat a majority-class (<<f3(MAJ['pr_auc'])>>) and a rule-of-thumb baseline (<<f3(RULE['pr_auc'])>>), but their 95\% confidence intervals overlap: at this decision time gradient boosting does not significantly beat a regularised linear model. We show why: early stopping on the scored month inflates boosting's validation score, the useful tree count is unstable between months, and tree splits on absolute delay-rate levels suffer from seasonal drift. An appendix records every design decision, the evidence for it and what happened.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Late arrivals cascade through crews, gates and connections. A risk score available before the first departure lets planners add buffer or warn passengers. Following the course's five-step blueprint, the goal is to rank flights by delay risk (PR AUC) and to produce a flag list with precision $\geq 0.5$. The problem is binary classification: the U.S.\ BTS defines a flight as delayed when it arrives 15 or more minutes late, and delay minutes are heavily skewed (skewness <<f2(d2['arr_delay_skew'])>>). The project brief asks for gradient boosting; we test whether it is justified against simple baselines, as Phase 7 of the life cycle requires. The work is split across five notebooks, one per team member, each writing its own module in \texttt{src/fdp/} and saving its results to \texttt{reports/}, from which this report is generated.

