\section{Problem, Data and Split (Phases 1, 2, 5)}
\textbf{Decision time.} Inputs must be known at 05:00 on day D: the published schedule for D, static aircraft facts and outcomes of earlier days. We first considered ``end of the previous day'', but about 3\% of the previous day's flights land after midnight, so 05:00 is when that history is really complete. Departure and arrival times, delays and air time are never inputs.

\textbf{Data.} The Kaggle \emph{Flights} (nycflights13) tables contain <<n(d1['summary']['flights'])>> scheduled departures from JFK, LGA and EWR plus a planes table. <<n(d1['summary']['cancelled'])>> cancelled and <<n(d1['summary']['diverted'])>> diverted flights have no arrival delay, so their label is undefined; they are kept on the schedule (they count towards congestion) but never labelled, leaving <<n(d1['summary']['labelled'])>> labelled flights, <<f3(d1['summary']['delayed_share_of_labelled'])>> of them delayed.

\textbf{Metric first.} The metric was fixed before any model: PR AUC (average precision), with ROC AUC, Brier score, expected calibration error (ECE), lift = PR AUC / base rate, and 95\% confidence intervals from a day-block bootstrap (flights on one day share weather, so days are resampled). Accuracy is rejected: predicting ``always on time'' scores <<f3(d1['valid_always_on_time_accuracy'])>> accuracy in September and catches no delays.

\textbf{Split.} The split is chronological (Table~\ref{tab:split}); January 1--14 only feeds history features. The monthly delay rate moves from <<f3(min(d1['monthly_delay_rate'].values()))>> (September) to <<f3(max(d1['monthly_delay_rate'].values()))>> (December, Fig.~\ref{fig:split}). This base-rate shift recurs in every later result.

