\section{EDA and Preprocessing (Phases 3, 4)}
All EDA uses the training block only. Aircraft year is missing for <<d2['missing_pct']['plane_year']>>\% of flights, concentrated in MQ (<<d2['plane_year_missing_by_carrier_top']['MQ']>>\%) and AA (<<d2['plane_year_missing_by_carrier_top']['AA']>>\%), whose tail numbers are absent from the planes table. Because this is systematic, deleting rows would bias two airlines; we impute the training median and add a missing-indicator column. <<f2(100 * d2['iqr_outlier_share'])>>\% of arrival delays are IQR outliers; they are real and cannot distort a yes/no label, so they are kept. There are <<d2['duplicates_full']>> duplicates. The delay rate rises from <<f3(d2['delay_rate_by_hour']['5'])>> at 05:00 to <<f3(d2['delay_rate_by_hour']['20'])>> at 20:00 (delay propagation), differs more than twofold between carriers, and is lowest on Saturdays (Fig.~\ref{fig:eda}).

The daily delay rate has lag-1 autocorrelation <<d2['acf']['lag1']>>, a PACF cut-off after lag 1 (lag-2 PACF <<d2['pacf']['lag2']>>) and lag-7 autocorrelation <<d2['acf']['lag7']>>; the ADF test rejects a unit root ($p<10^{-10}$). Yesterday therefore carries most of the memory (a 1-day history window), and the weekly pattern is a fixed day-of-week effect (calendar features), not weekly memory. Longer 7- and 28-day windows are used only for small groups whose daily rates are noisy.

With <<f3(d2['train_delay_rate'])>> of training flights delayed, imbalance is moderate, so we do not re-sample (it distorts probabilities). Linear models get median imputation, scaling and one-hot encoding (levels with $<50$ training flights grouped); trees get ordinal codes and no scaling; XGBoost uses native categorical splits. A standard and a robust scaler tied on September (PR AUC <<d2['scaler_comparison']['standard']['valid_pr_auc']>> vs <<d2['scaler_comparison']['robust']['valid_pr_auc']>>); the standard scaler is kept. Every statistic is fitted inside a scikit-learn pipeline on training rows only.

