\section{What Drives the Model}
Importance and ablation use October, never the test set. By permutation importance boosting relies most on scheduled hour (<<d5['permutation_top12']['sched_hour']>> drop in PR AUC) and the flight number's 28-day delay rate (<<d5['permutation_top12']['h_flight_28d_rate']>>). Adding feature groups to boosting one at a time, October PR AUC peaks at <<f3(abl['+ aircraft'])>> with schedule, calendar and aircraft, falls to <<f3(abl['+ congestion'])>> with congestion and to <<f3(abl['+ history'])>> with history, while logistic regression gained from both groups. Trees split on absolute levels (``28-day rate $> 0.31$'') learned in a high-delay summer; in calmer autumn months those thresholds sit in the wrong place, whereas a linear model only rescales the rate. The same drift shows by slice: boosting's lift is <<lift['November']['Gradient boosting']>> in November against <<lift['November']['Logistic regression']>> for logistic regression, and December is hard for every model (about <<lift['December']['Logistic regression']>>).

