\section{Discussion and Threats to Validity}
The decision log in the appendix lists all <<len(LOG)>> decisions and their outcomes. Most protective decisions did what they were meant to: the chronological split and day-block intervals prevented an inflated or over-confident claim, the two-month veto stopped SFS from shrinking the feature set, and the honest protocol removed a +<<f3(opt['mean'] - hon['mean'])>> optimism from boosting's tuning score. Limitations: (i) $C=<<d4['best_C']>>$ was chosen on September and then reused in the rolling comparison whose September fold is the same month (<<f4(cv['Logistic regression']['Sep'])>> vs <<f4(ms4['Logistic regression']['Sep'])>> with $C=1$), and boosting's configuration was likewise picked on the folds it is reported on, so both rolling means are slightly optimistic; the test set is unaffected. (ii) The search covered <<len(search)>> of <<n_combos>> configurations, although their spread was small. (iii) The hybrid's August fold stopped at <<d5['hybrid_trees_per_fold'][1]>> trees against <<d5['hybrid_trees_per_fold'][0]>> and <<d5['hybrid_trees_per_fold'][2]>> in the other folds; the median rule absorbs this, but it shows how unstable the transferable correction is. (iv) Ablation is based on one month and a fixed tree count. (v) One year of data cannot separate season from trend. (vi) Notebooks ran on different machines (notebook 04: Python <<d4['run']['python']>>, scikit-learn <<d4['run']['scikit_learn']>>, XGBoost <<d4['run']['xgboost']>>); re-runs may differ in the third decimal.

\section{Conclusion}
At a 05:00 decision time, gradient boosting does not significantly outperform a regularised logistic regression; both clearly beat simple baselines, and a hybrid that boosts from the linear prediction matches the linear model. The useful information at 05:00 is mostly recent delay history, which a linear model uses as well as trees and transfers better across seasons. Honest tuning matters: a common early-stopping shortcut would have overstated boosting. Future work: drift-robust (relative) history features, information known at 05:00 such as weather forecasts and planned traffic programmes, more years of data, and temporal deep learning in Phase 2. \fillme{Phase 2 plan.}

